# Control of Metal-Organic Framework Crystallization by Metastable Intermediate Pre-equilibrium Species

## abstract
There is an increasing amount of interest in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for a variety of applications, from gas sensing and separations to electronics and catalysis. However, the mechanisms by which they crystallize remain poorly understood. Herein, an important new insight into MOF formation is reported. It is shown that, prior to network assembly, crystallization intermediates in the canonical ZIF-8 system exist in a dynamic pre-equilibrium, which depends on the reactant concentrations and the progress of reaction. Concentration can, therefore, be used as a synthetic handle to directly control particle size, with potential implications for industrial scale-up and gas sorption applications. These findings enable the rationalization of apparent contradictions between previous studies of ZIF-8 and opens up new opportunities for the control of crystallization in network solids more generally.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) show exceptional promise for gas separation,[1] sensing,[2] catalysis,[3] and other applications, owing to their modular, porous architecture based on metal nodes and organic linkers.[4,5] The processes of MOF nucleation and crystal growth are fundamentally important to particle size and shape,[6] yield and efficiency in industrial scale-up,[7] and phase selection.[8] Yet, despite increasing awareness, the crystallization of MOFs is poorly understood.[9,10] ZIF-8, $\left[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{mIm})_{2}\right]$ $\mathrm{\mImH}=2$ -methylimidazole), shows potential for carbon capture, propene/propane separations, and dielectric applications[11] and is one of the few MOFs that have been investigated in detail as a model system for MOF crystallization.[12–19] ZIF-8 nanoparticles were first synthesized by Cravillon et al. in 2009, using excess mImH to deprotonate linkers and terminate particle surfaces.[12] Static light scattering revealed slow, continuous nucleation, and fast crystal growth.[14] In situ X-ray diffraction (XRD) suggested that the crystallization process is phaseboundary-limited,[20,21] as evidenced by the Avrami exponent, $n$ ,[22–24] taking values of $n\approx1$ .[25] However, other XRD measurements reported values ranging from $n=0.6$ (diffusion-limited) in methanol:aqueous ammonia,[26] to $n=1$ in heated $\mathrm{DMF}^{[27]}$ and $n=4$ (classical, 3D monomer attachment) in pure methanol.[28] Small-angle $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray scattering (SAXS) showed that clusters approximately $1\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter associate to form intermediate amorphous particles.[15] Electrospray ionization mass spectrometry (ESI-MS) indicated that several different clusters co-exist in the reaction solution.[16] However, in situ atomic force microscopy (AFM) revealed that the key species at the crystal surface are individual mImH linkers and solvated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions.[17] On the other hand, a recent total scattering investigation suggested that the solution state is dominated by $\mathrm{[Zn(mImH)_{4}]}$ -like species.[19] The apparent disparity between these observations indicates that we are far from understanding the chemistry of MOF crystal nucleation and growth, even in such an ostensibly simple system as ZIF-8.
Herein, a tandem in situ XRD and $\mathrm{pH}$ investigation into ZIF-8 crystallization is reported in which it is shown that, under certain conditions, the rate of ZIF-8 crystallization decreases with increasing reactant concentration. To explain this counterintuitive observation, a model for ZIF-8 crystallization is proposed, which involves a pre-equilibrium of metastable intermediate clusters with various Zn:mIm ratios and different degrees of protonation. The rate of crystallization depends on the position of this equilibrium: owing to favorable Zn-mIm coordination, crystallization from overstoichiometric clusters is dissociative and slower than crystallization from under-stoichiometric clusters. By considering how the pre-equilibrium is perturbed by changes in concentration, we can rationalize the apparent contradictions between previous observations and identify new ways for controlling ZIF-8 crystal size and structure.
In situ $\mathbf{XRD}^{[29-33]}$ data were collected, on Beamline I12, at Diamond Light Source,[34] on a series of room-temperature reactions in methanol in which the $\mathrm{mImH}{:}Z{\mathrm{n}} $ ratio was kept constant at 4:1 and the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ concentration, $[Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}]_{0}$ , was varied from $0.02{\bf{M}}$ to $0.10\mathbf{M}$ in $0.02\mathbf{M}$ steps. Under these conditions, the crystallization rate is observed to decrease with increasing concentration of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ (Figure 1a,b). Integrated XRD peak intensities were extracted using Pawley refinements (see Sections S1 and S2 in the Supporting Information) and their changes were fitted to the expression of Gualtieri[35] to give the rates of nucleation, $k_{\mathrm{N}},$ from $5.57(7)\times10^{-3}{\bf s}^{-1}$ to $7.9(4)\times10^{-4}\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ (for $[Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}]_{0}=0.02\mathrm{{M}}$ to $0.10\mathbf{M})$ and growth, $k_{\mathrm{G}},$ from $2.1(4)\times10^{-3}{\bf s}^{-1}$ to $2.67(9)\times$ $10^{-4}{\bf s}^{-1}$ , confirming that both nucleation and crystal growth are inhibited as concentration increases (Figure 1c). The single exponent, $n$ , given by the Gualtieri expression,[35] ranges from 0.47(4) to 1.03(6), without an obvious trend with concentration. However, analysis by the Sharp–Hancock method[21] shows that $n$ changes with time, from $n\approx1$ in the initial stages to $n<1$ in the later stages, suggesting a progression from phase boundary conditions to diffusion-limited crystallization (Supporting Information, Figure S28).
