The important consequence of this model is that the crystallization rate and mechanism do not depend directly on the concentration of reagents, but on the position of preequilibrium: when under-stoichiometric species $(x+y<2)$ dominate, crystallization is associative and fast, whereas when over-stoichiometric species dominate, crystallization is limited by the rate of mImH dissociation and is therefore slower. Simulations in COPASI[39] show that before pre-equilibrium is attained, under-coordinated species prevail (Figure 3a), resulting in associative, nucleation-dominated crystallization (Figure 3b). However, once pre-equilibrium is reached, clusters become over-coordinated, resulting in a reduction in nucleation relative to growth. At higher concentration, nucleation is initially faster (Figure 3 d), but pre-equilibrium is achieved faster and is pushed further towards overcoordinated species (Figure 3 c), leading to a stronger suppression of crystallization overall (Figure 3e). Sharp–Hancock analysis suggests that changes in n occur upon changes in the crystallization route (i.e. nucleation vs. growth), relative concentrations of intermediates, and $\mathrm{pH}$ (see Figure S29 in a decrease in the concentration of neutral mImH species $(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}=14.2$ in water)[40] owing to rapid formation of $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ - $\mathrm{(mImH)}_{\mathrm{3}}$ coordination complexes $\mathrm{\Deltap}K_{\mathrm{a}}=10.3$ in water),[41] and condensation of a few oligomers, which releases $\mathrm{mImH}_{2}^{+}$ . As pre-equilibrium is established, the rate of condensation decreases, leading to a plateau in $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ . Then autocatalytic crystal growth accelerates and is accompanied by the release of further $\mathrm{mImH}_{2}^{+}$ . The resulting second decrease in $\mathrm{\pH}$ exhibits the same timescale as crystal growth observed by in situ XRD (see Figure S33 in the Supporting Information), which suggests that the condensation and crystallization processes are linked. If the rate of crystal growth is related to diffusion of $\mathrm{mImH}_{2}^{+}$ , this would be consistent with a value of $n<1$ found towards the end of the reactions. Evidence that the pre-equilibrium exists in solution, rather than in amorphous particles, comes from in situ measurements of turbidity, which show time and concentration dependence, similar to nucleation, as measured by XRD (see Figure S33 in the Supporting Information). That is, condensation to form particles occurs during the second $\mathrm{pH}$ decrease and not before. Ex situ scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images show that particle diameter increases with increasing concentration (Figure 5), somewhat in contrast to the expectation of classical, linear crystallization. The preequilibrium model explains why this is the case: with increasing concentration, the number of nuclei produced remains constant but, due to increased reactant availability, the average particle size increases (Figure 5, inset).
Although the simplicity of the condensation process in our model precludes direct fitting to what are undoubtedly very complex crystallization curves, which arise from, for example, surface area and diffusion effects, we emphasize here that the trends in our data are qualitatively well captured. The preequilibrium model also helps to rationalize the diversity of previous observations. Highly basic solutions, for example, a large excess of mImH or $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ ,[12,14–16,26] promote deprotonation and a fast, associative route. Low values of $n$ reflect the diffusion limit of such rapid crystallization. Increasing concentration favors over-coordinated species; the model correctly predicts the dominance of $\mathrm{[Zn(mImH)_{4}]}$ -like species in such crystallizations.[19] The use of water in the same study also slowed crystallization, via faster coordination, which allowed pre-equilibrium conditions to be more rapidly reached: solvent–ligand exchange at labile ions, such as $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , is limited by solvent dissociation and water dissociation is known to be at least an order of magnitude faster than methanol dissociation.[42] Finally, in extremely dilute, mildly acidic conditions, the pre-equilibrium favors low coordination numbers and even isolated mImH and $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathbf{S})_{z}]^{2+}$ species (see Figure 3a at extended times), resulting in extremely slow crystallization and phase boundary conditions.[17]
The qualitative importance of $\mathrm{pH}$ in the thermodynamics and kinetics of MOF formation is already widely known.[43] For example, the synthesis of many MOFs requires decomposition of DMF to release dimethylamine, which acts as a pH modifier to induce nucleation. On the other hand, absolute concentration has rarely been used as a synthetic handle to control MOF formation. These results show that, somewhat counterintuitively, increasing concentration can inhibit nucleation by promoting over-coordination, and lead to changes in the growth mechanism. Whilst further verification of the preequilibrium model could be demonstrated by performing in situ ESI-MS, SAXS or transmission electron microscopy (TEM), these results provide a new framework in which to interpret MOF syntheses, predict the effects of tuning different reaction parameters, and relate them to the solution chemistry of the metal and linkers. For instance, pH control at high concentrations represents an obvious means to inhibit nucleation of metastable precursor solutions, prior to seeding or addition of base, to control particle size or film thickness. Furthermore, by diversifying the composition of pre-equilibrium species, for example, via dynamic combinatorial chemistry, we may be able to access new phases with specific compositions, local structures, or correlated disorder.
